const dynamicPages=[`<div class="title-container"></div><div class="credits">CONFRONTING THE UNKNOWABLE<br>NATURE OF THE NOUMENON<br><br><span style="color:#e5d1b3; font-size:1.2rem;">BY Lazyman_XD</span></div>`,`<div class="title-container" style="margin: 0 0 20px 0; text-align:left;"><div class="title-large" style="font-size: 2.2rem; letter-spacing:1px;">THE GOD ⟂</div><div class="title-combined" style="justify-content:flex-start;"><div class="drop-n" style="font-size: 3.8rem;">N</div><div class="title-sub-text" style="font-size: 2rem; margin-top: 4px; letter-spacing:1px;">OUMENON</div></div></div><p>The observer existed even before the very concept of observation came to mind—one God. A nameless god, for a god has no concept of names, but is a keeper of structures. For three thousand years, he had worn the skin of countless travelers, wandering the ridges of the valleys simply to watch the mortals build. He took no pleasure in their wars or their loves, but he was endlessly fascinated by their hands—their endless knowledge of both the known and the unknown.</p><p>It was curiosity, cold and small, that made him approach the mason.</p><p>High on the mountain side, a man named John was building his new house. The stone foundations were halfway raised, cutting a straight, neat rectangle into the rugged slope. As the evening chill rolled over the ridge, John sat tight against his unfinished timber frame, stirring a pot of simple broth over a cooking fire.</p><p>Out of the dark tree line, a figure appeared.</p><p>John instantly went rigid, his hand tightening around the heavy wooden handle of his spade. He watched the man approach—a weary-looking traveler wrapped in a heavy, dust-laden woolen coat.</p>`,`<p>The stranger stopped just at the edge of the firelight, raising his hands slightly to show they were empty. "The wind is turning sharp on the ridge," the stranger said. His voice was unusually quiet, carrying a flat, hollow resonance. "May I camp near your fire for the night?"</p><p>John hesitated. He looked the man up and down, to see if the man was armed. his eyes darting to the dark trail behind him. It was a lonely place to meet anyone after dark. But the stranger looked exhausted, and the mountain winds were unforgiving.</p><p>John nodded slowly, though his posture remained guarded. "There's a flat log across from me. You can stay till dawn."</p><p>The stranger sat down, keeping his eyes fixed on the works of the mason.</p><p>To break the heavy, tense silence, John kept his eyes on his cooking pot and struck up a conversation, still trying to figure out who this man was. "A bit late to be trekking through the high woods, stranger. What brings a man up the mountain track overnight?"</p><p>The stranger didn't answer right away. He stared past the flames, his unblinking eyes locked onto the half-finished masonry cutting into the slope. "I was following the ridge," he said softly, his voice matching the low hum of the wind. "I wanted to see what was being built out here in the dark."</p><p>John let out a short, rough laugh, the tension in his shoulders easing just a bit. "Well, you found the right spot for it. I'm John, a mason by trade." He gestured with his wooden spoon toward the half-raised stone foundations behind them. "Building a new homestead right here. It's a brutal climb, but my wife... she fell in love with this ridge. She wanted a little farm up here, away from the crowded village square. Said she wanted a place where she could sit on the porch and watch the sunset over the valley every single evening without a wall blocking her view."</p><p>John smiled faintly at the thought, his mind completely insulated by the simple, beautiful future he was constructing with his hands. "So, I'm building it for her, simple heavy timber. A straight wall to keep the winter out."</p><p>"Have you ever experienced what the world is like outside of the walls, John?" the stranger asked. He leaned forward, his voice steady, rhythmic, and perfectly normal against the backdrop of the crackling fire.</p><p>John blinked, his smile faltering at the unexpected question from the stranger. "Outside the walls? Uhm—sure. I'm under the sky right now. I work under it every day."</p><p>"No," the stranger murmured, his voice dropping into a flat, rhythmic hum. "You John are inside a box made of blue air and sunlight,"</p>`,`<p>"You have never touched the outside. Your skin is too thick. Your eyes are too narrow to see the grain of reality."</p><p>John chuckled, though it was a nervous, hollow sound that died quickly in his throat. He shifted uncomfortably on his log, looking at the stranger's unblinking eyes, then down at his own calloused palms.</p><p>"The grain of... what?" John asked, his brow furrowing as a cold prickle of unease washed over his neck. "Look, friend, you're talking in riddles. My skin is thick because I handle rough granite all day. My eyes are just fine. I see the timber, I see the valley, and I see you sitting across from my fire eating my food."</p><p>The stranger didn't blink. He didn't even seem to breathe.</p><p>John's irritation began to override his confusion. He gripped the wooden handle of his spade a little tighter. "Are you drunk, or just trying to spook a man on his own land? If you're looking to mock my house or preach some eccentric philosophy, you can take your coat and march right back down the trail."</p><p>The stranger remained perfectly still, his silhouette bleeding into the shadows of the pines.</p><p>"Alright, stranger," John muttered, his voice dropping an octave as he began to rise. "I think it's about time you—"</p><p>He stopped. The warmth of the fire abruptly vanished from his skin. He reached up, his calloused hand brushing his nose, but he couldn't feel the texture of his flesh; it felt like sliding a hand over cold, smooth glass.</p><p>"Uhm... what's happening to me..." John stammered, his voice losing its edge, melting into a numb, confused whisper as his fingers scrambled over his own jaw.</p><p>"It is a quiet place," the stranger mused. "There is no weight there. No gravity to hold a house to the ground. Just the absolute foundation of the world."</p><p>John's heart hammered. He tried to stand, but his legs didn't register the pressure of the earth. He was becoming weightless. He looked down, but the world was graying at the edges, losing its color and depth, turning into a flat, washed-out sketch.</p><p>"The smell of the pines," the stranger continued, his voice sounding slightly more distant now, as if he were walking away. "The scent of the damp earth after a rain. Those are just creations, Elements, meant to keep you from noticing that you are standing on a void."</p><p>John tried to shout, but he couldn't feel his tongue or his throat. He clawed at his own face, his fingers sliding over a featureless expanse of skin where his mouth and nose should have been."</p>`,`<p>"No, no, no... Help me! Someone help me! I—"</p><p>The sound cut off. His fingers slid over smooth, unbroken skin. The world was shrinking to a tiny, pinpoint circle of vision.</p><p>"I envy you, in a way John," the stranger said. His voice was becoming thin, metallic, and faint, as if it were traveling through a long, vacuum-sealed pipe. "You get to believe the paint is the wall. You get to think the sunrise is a beginning, rather than just a shift in the light."</p><p>The last of John's sight winked out. The world collapsed into absolute, crushing blackness. He was a disembodied consciousness floating in a void, his own body nothing more than a memory.</p><p>"Listen, John," the stranger's voice whispered, so faint now it was barely a vibration in the dark. "The wind will continue to blow. But it has no sound. It is just... movement."</p><p>Then, the sound cut out. The stranger's voice vanished. The universe became a dead, silent absolute.</p><p>Seconds passed—or perhaps eons.</p><p>Then, the tide began to turn, but the return was broken. Through the heavy, suffocating dark, John's dead senses began to spark back to life one by one, Hitch and fractured.</p><p>First came a faint, rhythmic vibration, like the memory of a heartbeat. Then came the distant, ghostly smell of pine and damp earth. The darkness began to crack, revealing not the valley or the mountain, but a strange, reversed world—everything was backward, wrong.</p><p>John gasped, air rushing into lungs that felt brand new. He sat up, his hands grasping at the ground, but the ground felt like the sky. The sky felt like the ground. The world had flipped.</p><p>"You're back," the stranger's voice came from everywhere and nowhere at once. "But you're not the same, John. You've seen the outside now. You can never unsee it."</p><p>John looked around frantically. He was still on the mountain ridge, but everything was... different. The stars were burning with the cold, alien intensity of mathematical formulas. The wind spoke in shifting geometric patterns. The trees were not plants, but structures of pure light frozen in time.</p><p>"What have you done to me?" John whispered, his voice trembling.</p><p>"I have shown you the noumenon," the stranger replied. "The thing-in-itself. The reality behind the appearance. Now you understand why the mason builds. He builds because he knows, deep down, that the world is not what it seems."</p>`,`<p>John stared at his hands. They were the same hands, calloused and strong, but now he could see through them—see the atoms dancing, the electrons spinning, the empty space between everything. He could see the structure.</p><p>"How do I go back?" John asked, panic rising in his chest. "How do I become normal again?"</p><p>"You don't," the stranger said softly. "But you can learn to live with it. You can learn to build like the mason, to create structures that acknowledge the truth while still providing shelter from it."</p><p>John looked at his half-finished house. It was no longer just a house. It was a geometry of protection, a mathematical defense against the void. He understood now.</p><p>"You're not just a traveler, are you?" John said quietly.</p><p>"No," the stranger replied. "I am the observer. I am the one who watches the structure, who maintains the balance between the known and the unknown. And now, John, you are one of us."</p><p>The stranger turned and began to walk back into the dark tree line. But as he walked, he didn't disappear into the shadows—he dissolved into them, becoming one with the mathematical patterns of the night.</p><p>John stood alone on the mountain ridge, the cold wind howling around him. But he no longer felt the cold. He no longer felt fear. He felt only the quiet, terrible beauty of the structure.</p><p>He picked up his spade. He returned to his work. He built.</p>`,`<p>Days turned into weeks, weeks into months. John's wife never questioned why he had changed, why he worked in silence, why he stared at the empty air as if seeing things that weren't there. She simply accepted it, as wives do, as mortals do.</p><p>The house was completed. It stood as a perfect rectangle against the mountain slope, a defiance of the chaos, a structure of order in a world of shifting forms.</p><p>John lived there with his family. He watched his children grow. He watched the seasons change. But always, in the back of his mind, he could see the noumenon—the reality behind the appearance, the mathematical truth beneath the surface.</p><p>Sometimes, late at night, he would step out onto the porch and look up at the stars. And sometimes, just sometimes, he would see a figure standing at the edge of the tree line—a weary traveler in a dust-laden coat, watching him with eyes that held no judgment, only understanding.</p><p>John would nod. The figure would nod back. And then the night would continue, the wind would blow, and the structure would hold.</p><p>This was his life now. This was his truth. He was a mason, a builder, a keeper of structures. He had seen the outside, and he had returned to build a house in the inside.</p><p>And that, he supposed, was enough.</p>`,`<p>The years passed like pages turning in a book—swift, inevitable, carrying stories within stories. John grew old, his hands gnarled from decades of labor, his hair turning the color of mountain stone.</p><p>His children grew up and moved away, as children do. His wife grew old beside him, her face becoming a map of the life they had built together on this ridge.</p><p>The house remained—solid, enduring, perfect in its geometry. It had become more than a home; it was a monument to the balance between the known and the unknown, a testament to a man who had seen the void and chosen to build within it anyway.</p><p>On the day John died, he sat on the porch for the last time. The wind was blowing, cold and sharp, just as it had on that night so many years ago. The stars were burning with their mathematical intensity.</p><p>He closed his eyes and felt the structure one last time—the atoms, the electrons, the empty space, the beautiful terrible truth of existence.</p><p>When he opened his eyes again, the figure was there. Not at the edge of the tree line, but standing right beside him on the porch.</p><p>"It's time," the stranger said.</p><p>John nodded. He had known this was coming. He had felt it in his bones, in the structure of his cells, in the mathematical pattern of his heartbeat.</p><p>"Will I see the outside again?" John asked.</p><p>"You never left it," the stranger replied. "You've been building in it all along."</p><p>John smiled. He stood up, his old body feeling light as air, and walked toward the figure. Together, they stepped off the porch and into the night—into the shifting geometric patterns, into the mathematical truth, into the noumenon itself.</p><p>The house remained on the mountain ridge, solid and enduring, waiting for the next builder, the next observer, the next one who would see the structure and choose to build within it anyway.</p>`,`<div class="blurb-container"><div class="blurb-title">WHAT LIES BEYOND THE AIR WE BREATHE?</div><div class="blurb-text">John was a simple stone mason, building a quiet home for his family on a rugged mountain ridge. But a late-night traveler brings a cold, terrifying perspective to his hearth.</div><div class="blurb-text">When the veil of human perception is peeled back, what remains is not a sanctuary under the sky, but an unforgiving void of absolute mathematics and shifting shapes. Once you glimpse the noumenon, can you ever truly feel at home inside the box again?</div></div><div class="back-footer"><div class="publisher-info"><strong>VOIDWEAVER PRESS</strong><br>Fiction / Philosophical Horror</div><div class="barcode"><div class="barcode-lines"></div><div class="barcode-text">978-0-1234-5678-9</div></div></div>`];

let isMobileDevice = false;
let currentSheetIndex = 0;
let sheetsMap = new Map();
let totalSheets = 0;

// --- NEW: separate, smaller intrinsic canvas for mobile -----------------
// The old code always laid the book out at 1240x1754px and only shrank it
// visually with a CSS transform: scale(). The browser still has to
// rasterize every page/box-shadow/3D layer at that full 1240x1754
// resolution before the scale is applied, which is the main reason the
// book felt so heavy on phones. Rendering a much smaller canvas on mobile
// (that keeps the same aspect ratio) means there are far fewer pixels for
// the GPU to composite every time a page flips.
const DESKTOP_PAGE_WIDTH = 1240;
const DESKTOP_PAGE_HEIGHT = 1754;
const MOBILE_PAGE_WIDTH = 680;   // same 0.707 aspect ratio as desktop
const MOBILE_PAGE_HEIGHT = 962;

let book, bookScaler, zoneLeft, zoneRight, btnPrev, btnNext, exitScreen, reopenBtn, navArrows;
let scaleX, scaleY;

function initializeElements() {
  book = document.getElementById('book');
  bookScaler = document.getElementById('book-scaler');
  zoneLeft = document.getElementById('zone-left');
  zoneRight = document.getElementById('zone-right');
  btnPrev = document.getElementById('btn-prev');
  btnNext = document.getElementById('btn-next');
  exitScreen = document.getElementById('exit-screen');
  reopenBtn = document.getElementById('reopen-btn');
  navArrows = document.getElementById('nav-arrows');

  zoneRight.addEventListener('click', flipNext);
  zoneLeft.addEventListener('click', flipPrev);
  btnNext.addEventListener('click', flipNext);
  btnPrev.addEventListener('click', flipPrev);
  reopenBtn.addEventListener('click', reopenBook);

  // btn-prev/btn-next are role="button" divs (not real <button> elements),
  // so Enter/Space activation has to be wired up manually for keyboard
  // and screen-reader users.
  const activateOnKey = (fn) => (e) => {
    if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' ') {
      e.preventDefault();
      fn();
    }
  };
  btnNext.addEventListener('keydown', activateOnKey(flipNext));
  btnPrev.addEventListener('keydown', activateOnKey(flipPrev));
}

function renderBookStructure() {
  if (!book) return;

  const clickLayer = document.querySelector('.click-layer');
  book.innerHTML = '';
  if (clickLayer) book.appendChild(clickLayer);
  sheetsMap.clear();

  const existingBookmark = document.getElementById('bookmark-exit');
  if (existingBookmark) existingBookmark.closest('.bookmark-anchor').remove();

  const bookmarkHTML = `<div class="bookmark-anchor"><div class="bookmark-ribbon" id="bookmark-exit" title="Exit Book"><span class="bookmark-text">EXIT</span></div></div>`;
  book.insertAdjacentHTML('beforeend', bookmarkHTML);

  // NEW: set the intrinsic canvas size to match device class, instead of
  // always laying out at the full desktop resolution.
  const w = isMobileDevice ? MOBILE_PAGE_WIDTH : DESKTOP_PAGE_WIDTH;
  const h = isMobileDevice ? MOBILE_PAGE_HEIGHT : DESKTOP_PAGE_HEIGHT;
  bookScaler.style.width = w + 'px';
  bookScaler.style.height = h + 'px';
  book.style.width = w + 'px';
  book.style.height = h + 'px';

  if (isMobileDevice) {
    totalSheets = dynamicPages.length;
    for (let idx = 0; idx < totalSheets; idx++) {
      const pageEl = document.createElement('div');
      pageEl.className = 'page';
      pageEl.dataset.index = idx;

      const contentHtml = dynamicPages[idx];
      const isCover = idx === 0;
      const isBack = idx === totalSheets - 1;
      const frontClass = isCover ? 'front hardcover' : 'front';
      const pageLabel = (isCover || isBack) ? '' : `Page ${idx}`;

      pageEl.innerHTML = `<div class="${frontClass}"><div class="content">${contentHtml}</div><div class="page-number">${pageLabel}</div></div><div class="back ${isBack ? 'hardcover' : ''}"></div>`;
      pageEl.style.zIndex = totalSheets - idx;
      book.appendChild(pageEl);
      sheetsMap.set(idx, pageEl);
    }
  } else {
    totalSheets = Math.ceil(dynamicPages.length / 2);
    for (let i = 0; i < totalSheets; i++) {
      const pageEl = document.createElement('div');
      pageEl.className = 'page';
      pageEl.dataset.index = i;

      const frontPageIndex = 2 * i;
      const backPageIndex = 2 * i + 1;
      const isFrontCover = i === 0;
      const isBackCover = i === totalSheets - 1;
      const isFrontBackCover = frontPageIndex === dynamicPages.length - 1;
      const isBackBackCover = backPageIndex === dynamicPages.length - 1;
      const frontClass = isFrontCover ? 'front hardcover' : 'front';
      const backClass = (isBackCover || isBackBackCover) ? 'back hardcover' : 'back';
      const frontContent = dynamicPages[frontPageIndex] || '';
      const backContent = dynamicPages[backPageIndex] || '';
      const frontNum = (frontPageIndex > 0 && frontPageIndex < dynamicPages.length - 1) ? frontPageIndex : '';
      const backNum = (backPageIndex > 0 && backPageIndex < dynamicPages.length - 1) ? backPageIndex : '';

      pageEl.innerHTML = `<div class="${frontClass}"><div class="content">${frontContent}</div><div class="page-number" style="text-align:right;">${frontNum}</div></div><div class="${backClass}"><div class="content">${backContent}</div><div class="page-number" style="text-align:left;">${backNum}</div></div>`;
      pageEl.style.zIndex = totalSheets - i;
      book.appendChild(pageEl);
      sheetsMap.set(i, pageEl);
    }
  }

  if (currentSheetIndex >= totalSheets) currentSheetIndex = totalSheets - 1;
  attachBookmarkListener();
  updateBookState();
}

function attachBookmarkListener() {
  const bookmarkExit = document.getElementById('bookmark-exit');
  if (!bookmarkExit) return;
  const newBookmarkExit = bookmarkExit.cloneNode(true);
  bookmarkExit.parentNode.replaceChild(newBookmarkExit, bookmarkExit);
  newBookmarkExit.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
    e.preventDefault(); e.stopPropagation(); e.stopImmediatePropagation();
    exitBook();
  });
  newBookmarkExit.addEventListener('touchend', (e) => {
    e.preventDefault(); e.stopPropagation(); e.stopImmediatePropagation();
    exitBook();
  });
  newBookmarkExit.addEventListener('mousedown', (e) => {
    e.preventDefault(); e.stopPropagation();
  });
}

function rescaleBook() {
  if (!bookScaler) return;

  const windowW = window.innerWidth;
  const windowH = window.innerHeight;
  const wasMobile = isMobileDevice;
  isMobileDevice = windowW < 850;

  // Only rebuild the DOM when crossing the mobile/desktop boundary, not on
  // every resize/orientation tick.
  if (wasMobile !== isMobileDevice) renderBookStructure();

  const pageW = isMobileDevice ? MOBILE_PAGE_WIDTH : DESKTOP_PAGE_WIDTH;
  const pageH = isMobileDevice ? MOBILE_PAGE_HEIGHT : DESKTOP_PAGE_HEIGHT;
  const spreadW = isMobileDevice ? pageW : pageW * 2;

  let scale;
  if (isMobileDevice) {
    document.body.classList.add('is-mobile');
    scaleX = (0.95 * windowW) / spreadW;
    scaleY = (0.9 * windowH) / pageH;
    scale = Math.min(scaleX, scaleY);
  } else {
    document.body.classList.remove('is-mobile');
    scaleX = (0.85 * windowW) / spreadW;
    scaleY = (0.85 * windowH) / pageH;
    scale = Math.min(scaleX, scaleY);
  }

  bookScaler.style.transform = `scale(${scale})`;
  updateBookState();
}

// NEW: only the sheets actually mid-flip get a will-change hint. Every
// other sheet (most of the book, most of the time) is left as a normal,
// cheap layer instead of being permanently promoted to its own GPU layer.
function markActiveSheets() {
  sheetsMap.forEach((sheet, i) => {
    const isNearActive = Math.abs(i - currentSheetIndex) <= 1;
    sheet.classList.toggle('will-flip', isNearActive);
  });
}

function updateBookState() {
  if (!book || !btnPrev || !btnNext) return;

  book.classList.remove('closed-front', 'open-spread', 'closed-back');
  if (!isMobileDevice) {
    if (currentSheetIndex === 0) book.classList.add('closed-front');
    else if (currentSheetIndex === totalSheets - 1) book.classList.add('closed-back');
    else book.classList.add('open-spread');
  }

  for (let i = 0; i < totalSheets; i++) {
    const sheet = sheetsMap.get(i);
    if (!sheet) continue;
    if (i < currentSheetIndex) {
      sheet.classList.add('flipped');
      sheet.style.zIndex = i + 1;
    } else {
      sheet.classList.remove('flipped');
      sheet.style.zIndex = totalSheets - i;
    }
  }

  markActiveSheets();

  btnPrev.classList.toggle('disabled', currentSheetIndex === 0);
  btnNext.classList.toggle('disabled', currentSheetIndex >= totalSheets - 1);
}

function flipNext() {
  if (currentSheetIndex < totalSheets - 1) {
    currentSheetIndex++;
    updateBookState();
  }
}

function flipPrev() {
  if (currentSheetIndex > 0) {
    currentSheetIndex--;
    updateBookState();
  }
}

function exitBook() {
  if (!bookScaler || !navArrows || !exitScreen) return;
  if (window.parent && window.parent !== window) {
    try {
      window.parent.postMessage('exit-infinite-book', '*');
      return;
    } catch (e) {}
  }
  bookScaler.style.opacity = '0';
  navArrows.style.opacity = '0';
  setTimeout(() => exitScreen.classList.add('active'), 300);
}

function reopenBook() {
  if (!exitScreen || !bookScaler || !navArrows) return;
  exitScreen.classList.remove('active');
  setTimeout(() => {
    bookScaler.style.opacity = '1';
    navArrows.style.opacity = '1';
  }, 200);
}

// NEW: lets the parent page reset the book to its cover when it re-opens
// a warmed/cached iframe (see the openInfiniteBook patch for index.html).
window.resetBookToStart = function resetBookToStart() {
  currentSheetIndex = 0;
  updateBookState();
};

let resizeTimeout;
window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
  clearTimeout(resizeTimeout);
  resizeTimeout = setTimeout(rescaleBook, 100);
});
window.addEventListener('orientationchange', () => setTimeout(rescaleBook, 100));
window.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
  if (e.key === 'ArrowRight') flipNext();
  if (e.key === 'ArrowLeft') flipPrev();
  if (e.key === 'Escape') exitBook();
});

if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    initializeElements();
    renderBookStructure();
    rescaleBook();
  });
} else {
  initializeElements();
  renderBookStructure();
  rescaleBook();
}